rows.solution:
First square A. Writing \(A = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 0 & 1 \\ 2 & 1 & 3 \\ 1 & -1 & 0 \end{bmatrix}\), each entry of \(A^2\) is a row of A dotted with a column of A:

row 1 gives \(4 + 0 + 1 = 5\), \(0 + 0 - 1 = -1\), \(2 + 0 + 0 = 2\);
row 2 gives \(4 + 2 + 3 = 9\), \(0 + 1 - 3 = -2\), \(2 + 3 + 0 = 5\);
row 3 gives \(2 - 2 + 0 = 0\), \(0 - 1 + 0 = -1\), \(1 - 3 + 0 = -2\).

So \(A^2 = \begin{bmatrix} 5 & -1 & 2 \\ 9 & -2 & 5 \\ 0 & -1 & -2 \end{bmatrix}\).

Next, \(5A = \begin{bmatrix} 10 & 0 & 5 \\ 10 & 5 & 15 \\ 5 & -5 & 0 \end{bmatrix}\) and \(4I = \begin{bmatrix} 4 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 4 \end{bmatrix}\).

Now combine entry by entry, \(A^2 - 5A + 4I\):
row 1: \(5 - 10 + 4 = -1\), \(-1 - 0 + 0 = -1\), \(2 - 5 + 0 = -3\);
row 2: \(9 - 10 + 0 = -1\), \(-2 - 5 + 4 = -3\), \(5 - 15 + 0 = -10\);
row 3: \(0 - 5 + 0 = -5\), \(-1 + 5 + 0 = 4\), \(-2 - 0 + 4 = 2\).

Hence \(A^2 - 5A + 4I = \begin{bmatrix} -1 & -1 & -3 \\ -1 & -3 & -10 \\ -5 & 4 & 2 \end{bmatrix}\). (Unlike the familiar textbook examples, this does not come out as the null matrix, so there is nothing to check against — the arithmetic itself is the answer.)
